itive, and all outcomes are non-positive, none are favorable. Thus, all probability mass is on unfavorable outcomes in both lotteries. But the reason says 'a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes'. Since favorable outcomes are absent in both, the portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes is 100% in both lotteries. Therefore, both have equal 'right-skewness' by this definition. Hence, the decision reason cannot distinguish between them based on skewness. Therefore, the reason is indifferent between the lotteries.",
